; Stage 15 W9 defect 21 + B2a — `(dyn P)` across a namespace, end to end, and
; the acceptance fixture for name resolution's front half.
;
; Two rulings are pinned here at once.
;
; W9 defect 21 made a protocol a NAMESPACED entity. Before it, `conformance-add`
; keyed the conformance under the BARE `Describe` (Stage 12 N4 stripped the
; qualifier off both the type and the protocol) while `protocol-lookup` matched
; the raw qualified spelling, so `(extend Fox Describe)` recorded a fact that
; `(dyn dp/Describe)` could never find — it reported
;   (dyn dp/Describe): 'dp/Describe' is not a declared protocol
; for a protocol that was both declared and conformed to.
;
; Stage 15 B2a (design/stage15-stress-test/name-resolution.md §8.3, R3/R7) then
; fixed WHICH SPELLINGS reach it. An import prefix binds the library under the
; prefix the consumer asked for and under nothing else; the library's own
; namespace is not in scope unless the file imported it by name. So this program
; spells the protocol `dpx/Describe` and `dpx2/Describe` — the prefixes its own
; import forms bind — and `dp/Describe` is now a hard error:
;   extend: unknown protocol 'dp/Describe'
;     note: 'dp' is not in scope in this file — a prefixed import binds its
;     library under the prefix it names and not under the library's own
;     namespace … In scope here: dpx, dpx2.
; (It is pinned as an error by tests/fixtures/b2a-ns-not-in-scope.nuc.) The
; earlier version of this file spelled `dp/`, and its header comment explained
; that an import prefix "aliases g-globals entries; it does not name protocols" —
; documenting the defect as if it were the design.
;
; What each line pins:
;   * `dpx/Describe` — a qualified protocol reference resolving through the
;     file's IMPORT ENVIRONMENT: the prefix names the namespace, and the two are
;     deliberately different (`dpx` vs `dp`), so this proves the resolution runs
;     through the prefix binding rather than through a global namespace list.
;   * `dpx/Fox` from lib/nsdescribe.nuc — its `(extend Fox Describe)` is written
;     BARE inside `(ns dp)`, the current-namespace path, so the bare and prefixed
;     spellings must land on one identity. Stage 15 B3′ (R1) made a TYPE name
;     namespaced too, so the constructor here is `dpx/Fox`: before B3′ a type was
;     bare-keyed and globally visible, and a plain `Fox` reached it from any file
;     under any qualifier (defects #4/#7). That bare spelling is now an error —
;     `unknown: Fox — not defined anywhere in this compilation unit` — and pinned
;     as one by tests/fixtures/b3-type-ns-not-in-scope.nuc.
;   * `Cat` conforming to BOTH `dpx/Describe` and `dpx2/Describe` — two protocols
;     that share the bare name `Describe` and do not collide; each box dispatches
;     its own protocol's method.
;
; Boxing happens here rather than in the libraries because a file with an
; explicit `(ns …)` cannot currently reach `default-allocator` — see the note in
; lib/nsdescribe.nuc.
;
; What this file does NOT pin, and was once misread as pinning (§9.4 → §9.5):
; the two box types it emits are for two DIFFERENT protocols in two different
; libraries, so two `{data,vtable}` StructDefs here are correct. Stage 15 B6
; changed only their NAMES — `%__dyn.dp_Describe` / `%__dyn.dp2_Describe` rather
; than the consumer's prefixes — because a box's identity is now the protocol's
; canonical name. The defect that renaming fixes is a cross-file one and cannot
; be witnessed inside a single file; `run_b6_dyn_cross_ns` in tests/run-tests.sh
; is the witness.

(import-use "stdio.h")
(import-use allocator)
(import-prefixed nsdescribe dpx)
(import-prefixed nsdescribe2 dpx2)

(defstruct Cat n:i32)

(defn describe ((self (ref Cat))):i32 (return (+ 100 (self n))))
(defn tag-of ((self (ref Cat))):i32 (return (+ 200 (self n))))

; Same bare protocol name, two namespaces, one conforming type.
(extend Cat dpx/Describe)
(extend Cat dpx2/Describe)

(defn main ():i32
  (let (a:(dyn dpx/Describe)  (Cat 5)
        b:(dyn dpx2/Describe) (Cat 7)
        c:(dyn dpx/Describe)  (dpx/Fox 9))
    (printf "%d\n" (describe a))
    (printf "%d\n" (tag-of b))
    ; `describe` is overloaded here (Cat and, from the library, Fox), so the
    ; call dispatches through the box's vtable — the Fox impl lives in namespace
    ; `dp` and is reached without naming it.
    (printf "%d\n" (describe c)))
  (return 0))
